{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-54998d1b-2145-452a-922e-701124172302","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-864008cab650778d","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-1gijbjoi/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E002.md","content":"# Standardize cloudmanager reconciliation error logs with structured resource context\n\n## Description\n\nApplies the same structured logging standardization to the cloudmanager binary\nthat ships with the rhods-operator. The cloudmanager binary handles multi-cloud\ninfrastructure dependencies (deployed on AWS EKS, Azure AKS, and CoreWeave) and\nreconciles the AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, and\nCoreWeaveKubernetesEngine resources through a shared reconciler pattern that\nalready provides `name` and `namespace` via controller-runtime's request logger.\nThis epic converts any remaining message-embedded resource identity in those\ncontrollers' error paths to structured key-value pairs and adds `resourceKind`\nso the infrastructure kinds are queryable like all manager-binary resources.\nThe work is in the same repository as the manager binary changes but is an\narchitecturally distinct sub-system with a narrower deployment footprint\n(non-OpenShift Kubernetes only), so it is separately schedulable.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit `log.Error()` call sites in the reconciliation error paths of the three\n  cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n  CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine).\n- Convert any error log that embeds resource name or namespace in the message\n  string to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`).\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager controller\n  reconciliation error logs (the kind is not implied by the shared reconciler\n  pattern, so this is a functional requirement, not just queryability).\n- Handle cluster-scoped infrastructure resources correctly: `name` and\n  `resourceKind` present, `namespace` omitted or empty — never a spurious value.\n- Extend the repository CI lint rule so it also covers the cloudmanager\n  controller paths once they are compliant.\n- Verify backwards compatibility for cloud deployments: before/after log output\n  comparison for a sample reconciliation error and confirmation that the\n  relevant log consumer (e.g. CloudWatch on cloud deployments) ingests both\n  formats without configuration changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Static analysis over the cloudmanager binary reports zero\n  reconciliation-error `log.Error` calls that embed resource name or namespace\n  in the message text.\n- Unit tests parsing test logger output assert `name`, `namespace`, and\n  `resourceKind` are present with correct values in the error logs of all three\n  cloudmanager controllers.\n- A unit test confirms cluster-scoped infrastructure resources emit `name` and\n  `resourceKind` without a spurious namespace value.\n- The repository CI lint rule covers the cloudmanager controller paths and\n  flags a deliberately non-compliant sample call in a test.\n- A before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error shows\n  the structured fields present, and the cloud log consumer ingests both the\n  old and new formats without configuration changes.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P0] All cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n  CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`,\n  `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n- [P0] All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or\n  namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs\n  (`name`, `namespace`) — cloudmanager binary scope.\n\n## Risks and Notes\n\n- Whether this work ships in the same release as the manager binary changes or\n  in a subsequent release is an open product decision; the epic is deliberately\n  independent so it can be scheduled either way without affecting the manager\n  work.\n- Cloudmanager is only deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure\n  AKS, CoreWeave), so its log-consumer regression surface is narrower than the\n  manager binary's.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":66793,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac712f216ff0358d","agent_id":"a4fb989e24f2e722c","session_id":"c024f609-bb75-4102-a6f5-d71433960ced","uuid":"9913469b-852f-4f83-bf31-24f584411eb1","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:18:44.013Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
