{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9fe2874ada462892","type":"tool_result","content":"16:**Detailed**: rhods-operator serves as the central control plane for Red Hat OpenShift AI (RHOAI). It defines and reconciles a hierarchy of custom resources—DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, and per-component CRDs such as DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, and KServe—that declaratively describe the desired state of the AI/ML platform. The operator deploys and manages each component's manifests via kustomize-based rendering and template actions, while a set of service controllers handle cross-cutting concerns: the Auth service controller manages RBAC group policies and watches for dependent namespaces (models-as-a-service, kuadrant-system), the Gateway service controller provisions a data-science gateway with kube-auth-proxy OIDC infrastructure and conditionally integrates with Istio (EnvoyFilter, DestinationRule) and OpenShift OAuth, and the Monitoring service controller deploys Prometheus proxies and collectors for platform-level observability. A companion cloudmanager binary handles cloud-provider Kubernetes engine resources (AWS, Azure, CoreWeave). The operator exposes 19 CRDs, serves admission webhooks on port 9443 with service-ca TLS, and reads the cluster APIServer TLS profile to align its own TLS configuration with the platform-wide security policy. [source: Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:56-59, cmd/main.go:480-494, internal/controller/services/auth/auth_controller.go:56-88, internal/controller/services/gateway/gateway_controller.go:40-109, pkg/tls/profile.go:151-162]\n20:rhods-operator follows a layered controller-runtime reconciliation architecture with three distinct controller tiers: a top-level DataScienceCluster controller that owns the platform lifecycle, per-component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) that manage individual AI/ML workload components, and service controllers (Auth, Gateway, Monitoring) that handle cross-cutting platform infrastructure.\n26:Security enforcement follows a defense-in-depth pattern: admission webhooks validate and default DataScienceCluster and DSCInitialization resources with a fail-closed policy, the metrics endpoint uses Kubernetes authentication and authorization filters when secure serving is enabled, and all webhook traffic is encrypted via OpenShift service-ca provisioned TLS certificates. The operator builds with `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` and `CGO_ENABLED=1`, ensuring FIPS-compliant cryptographic primitives on RHEL hosts. [source: Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:56-59, cmd/main.go:483-494]\n48:| cloudmanager | Go executable | cmd/cloudmanager |\n79:| dscinitialization.opendatahub.io | v1, v2 | DSCInitialization | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n82:| infrastructure.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | AWSKubernetesEngine | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n122:| github.com/blang/semver/v4 | v4.0.0 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n123:| github.com/go-logr/logr | v1.4.3 | Yes | runtime-observability | runtime-observability |\n124:| github.com/google/go-containerregistry | v0.21.8 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n125:| github.com/google/go-github/v67 | v67.0.0 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n126:| github.com/hashicorp/go-multierror | v1.1.1 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n127:| github.com/itchyny/gojq | v0.12.19 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n128:| github.com/k8s-manifest-kit/renderer-helm | v0.4.0 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n129:| github.com/mark3labs/mcp-go | v0.32.0 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n130:| github.com/onsi/ginkgo/v2 | v2.28.1 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n131:| github.com/onsi/gomega | v1.42.1 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n132:| github.com/opendatahub-io/models-as-a-service/maas-controller | v0.0.0-20260420142354-89fba298f42a | Yes |  | Go 