\"initially covering the manager binary paths\", while E002's scope says\n69\t\"Extend the repository CI lint rule so it also covers the cloudmanager\n70\tcontroller paths\" and E002's acceptance criteria require the lint rule to cover\n71\tcloudmanager paths and flag a non-compliant sample. E002 thus consumes and\n72\tmodifies an artifact produced by E001, yet the DAG declares the two epics\n73\tstrictly parallel with no edge, and the Rule 15 justification (\"same repo,\n74\tdifferent areas, parallel; merge coordination is a coordination risk, not a\n75\tdependency\") does not cover this shared artifact — as written, E002's\n76\tlint-rule scope item cannot be executed until E001's rule exists. This is a\n77\tcritical issue (missing DAG edge where an artifact dependency exists,\n78\tdiscoverable from epic content). Revision should either restructure the lint\n79\trule so it is not a cross-epic artifact (e.g., each epic owns lint coverage for\n80\tits own paths, or one epic creates the rule covering both binaries) or capture\n81\tthe ordering explicitly — adding a plain E001→E002 `dependencies` edge would\n82\tfix the inconsistency but serialize work that is otherwise parallel, so the\n83\trestructure is preferable.\n84\t\n85\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n86\tAll three epics carry the single canonical component (AI Core Platform, which\n87\tis in the canonical list; the non-obvious rhods-operator mapping is explicitly\n88\tlogged in the summary) and the single active team (ODH Operator team). The\n89\tmanager/cloudmanager split follows an architectural sub-system boundary with a\n90\treal scheduling motivation (the open release-timing question). Content\n91\tauthoring (E003) is correctly separated from code implementation rather than\n92\tbundled; E001's release-notes item is incidental documentation of a code\n93\tchange, not content authoring. No scope duplication between E001 and E002 —\n94\teach touches a distinct binary. Epic sizes track the strategy's S estimate\n95\t(~1-1.5 sprints for E001, ~0.25 for E002, ~0.5 for E003); nothing exceeds the\n96\t~2-week guideline.\n97\t\n98\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n99\tNo Investigation epics were created, and the assessment justifying that is\n100\tsound: each strategy open question was evaluated against \"does the answer\n101\tchange which downstream epics exist or what they do?\" and correctly routed to\n102\tan AC, a Health Warning, or in-epic verification work. Both implementation\n103\tepics produce concrete artifacts (code, tests, lint rule); E003 is correctly\n104\ttyped Implementation with `implementation_type: docs-authoring` and produces a\n105\tdocument deliverable. All `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are null,\n106\tconsistent with the absence of gates.\n107\t\n108\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n109\tAll three epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals` (9-signal\n110\trubric) with `investigation_signals: null`. Signal values are consistent with\n111\tthe strategy content and the rationales in the ai-signals files match the\n112\tfrontmatter values; E003's `open_questions: 0` and `external_dependency: 0` are\n113\thonest readings (the PM-owned additional-fields question is genuinely\n114\tunresolved and COO adoption gates nothing). One minor: E001's\n115\t`architecture_claims` rationale (and the decomposition summary) claims the\n116\tarchitecture context \"corroborates the five-controller-tier structure\", but the\n117\tactual context doc (`rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`) describes three\n118\tcontroller tiers and does not mention the module controller at all — the\n119\tfive-tier structure rests solely on the SME input. The signal value remains\n120\tdefensible (binaries, logr usage, CRDs, service and component controllers are\n121\tcorroborated), but the rationale overstates the corroboration.\n122\t\n123\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n124\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy's own\n125\tproposed ACs and verification methods. Rule-mandated ACs: the docs-authoring\n126\tepic has a documented technical review AC; no replacement or konflux-chain\n127\tepics exist, so those mandates do not apply. No non-testable schedule\n128\tconstraints are filed as ACs — the SRE/Support alert-rule coordination is\n129\tframed as a deliverable (inventory documented, rules updated), which is\n130\tverifiable. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID; cross-epic\n131\treferences use binary/scope language. Computed scores (`ai_implementability`)\n132\twere not checked, per the review contract.\n133\t\n134\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n135\tThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: all five manager\n136\tcontroller tiers plus 15 action files (E001), the three cloudmanager\n137\tcontrollers (E002), cluster-scoped resource handling in both binaries, the CI\n138\tlint rule, backwards-compatibility verification for both log-consumer stacks,\n139\tand the convention documentation with publication, COO tracking issues, and\n140\tthe additional-fields disposition (E003). Every strategy acceptance criterion\n141\thas a corresponding epic AC. Strategy-level context is carried forward: all\n142\tfour risks appear as epic ACs or Risks/Notes, and all four open questions are\n143\texplicitly dispositioned. One minor: the strategy's Supporting Documentation\n144\tlists \"Design doc: To be created\" (three-problem diagnosis, before/after\n145\texamples, DSCI migration guide); the decomposition folds that content into\n146\tE003's post-implementation contribution guideline without recording the\n147\tdisposition of the pre-implementation design doc — worth an explicit note, but\n148\tthe content itself is covered. No conditional branches are used, and none are\n149\tneeded (the only external unknown, COO adoption, gates nothing per overlay\n150\t0008, as the Tiered Delivery section correctly argues).\n151\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b94