{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b11f654f0b006f5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-8010 Decomposition — Operator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context\n11\t\n12\tThe strategy converts message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value\n13\tpairs (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) across rhods-operator reconciliation\n14\terror logs in both the manager and cloudmanager binaries, migrates the DSCI\n15\tcontroller off non-standard key names, and documents/publishes the convention.\n16\tIt decomposes into 3 epics: two parallel code Implementation epics (manager\n17\tbinary, cloudmanager binary) and one docs-authoring epic that documents and\n18\tpublishes the convention after the code lands.\n19\t\n20\t## Triage Assessment (Step 0)\n21\t\n22\tThe strategy is S-sized, affects a single component (rhods-operator), has a\n23\tsingle active team (ODH Operator team — the Cluster Observability Operator team\n24\tonly receives a published convention and does no work in this strategy), and\n25\twell over 67% of the scope is mechanical log-call conversion with high AI\n26\timplementability. However, the **multiple distinct work streams** escape\n27\tcondition applies: the scope spans (a) code implementation — auditing and\n28\tconverting `log.Error()` call sites across two operator binaries plus a CI lint\n29\trule — and (b) substantive content authoring — a new contribution guideline\n30\tdocumenting the structured log field convention (required fields, camelCase\n31\trationale, three-problem diagnosis, DSCI migration guide) and its publication\n32\tfor external operator adoption, which is a P1 HLR with its own acceptance\n33\tcriterion specifying the document's content. This is a new doc topic, not an\n34\tincidental touch-up. Full decomposition therefore applies.\n35\t\n36\t## Staff Engineer Input Handling (Step 1.5)\n37\t\n38\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Its\n39\tcorrections are already incorporated into the strategy body and are reflected in\n40\tthis decomposition:\n41\t\n42\t- Action file inventory corrected to exactly 15 files across four tiers\n43\t  (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module), not \"~16 component action files\".\n44\t- The module controller tier (module controller with 8 module handlers and its\n45\t  3 action files) is explicitly in scope and covered by the manager-binary epic.\n46\t- The wrong-premise open question about component action file counts is treated\n47\t  as answered (all 8 components have exactly one action file, confirmed 8/8) and\n48\t  does not generate an Investigation.\n49\t\n50\tNo divergence remains between the SME input and the AI-generated technical\n51\tapproach. The architecture context file for rhods-operator corroborates the\n52\tmanager/cloudmanager binaries, controller-runtime logr usage, and the DSC /\n53\tcomponent / service controller tiers; the five-tier structure (which adds the\n54\tmodule controller and DSCI tiers) is not described in the architecture context\n55\tand rests on the SME input alone.\n56\t\n57\t## Component Mapping\n58\t\n59\tThe strategy's single affected component, `rhods-operator`, has no verbatim\n60\tentry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. It is mapped to the canonical\n61\tcomponent **AI Core Platform** — a non-obvious mapping, logged here: the\n62\trhods-operator is the RHOAI platform's core control plane (per architecture\n63\tcontext, \"the RHOAI platform operator that orchestrates the lifecycle of all\n64\tOpenShift AI components\"), and no canonical component is named for the operator\n65\titself. All three epics span this one strategy component (manager binary,\n66\tcloudmanager binary, and repository-level convention documentation); each epic\n67\tis assigned the single most-relevant canonical name `AI Core Platform`.\n68\t\n69\t## Investigation Assessment (Step 3)\n70\t\n71\tAll unknowns were collected from the open questions table, risks, assumptions,\n72\tand pending process gates, then evaluated against the decision rule (*does the\n73\tanswer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?*):\n74\t\n75\t1. **Additional convention fields beyond name/namespace/kind** (owner: PM /\n76\t   Observability) — NO. The P0 HLRs fix the required field set\n77\t   (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`); the answer only affects optional\n78\t   content of the convention document. Captured as a scoped open question and an\n79\t   acceptance criterion on the docs epic (the guideline must record the explicit\n80\t   disposition of additional fields), not an Investigation.\n81\t2. **Which log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns** (owner: SRE /\n82\t   Support) — NO. The strategy states this is identified during the log consumer\n83\t   regression validation step before merge; it is verification work inside the\n84\t   implementations. Captured as an acceptance criterion on both implementation\n85\t   epics.\n86\t3. **Cloudmanager same release vs. subsequent release** (owner: PM / ODH\n87\t   Operator) — NO. The work is identical either way; only scheduling changes.\n88\t   Cloudmanager is structured as its own independently schedulable epic; the\n89\t   unresolved timing is recorded in Health Warnings.\n90\t4. **Actual percentage of embedded-identity log calls** (owner: ODH Operator) —\n91\t   NO. The audit is step 1 of the implementation itself; the conversion approach\n92\t   is unchanged regardless of the percentage. Captured as an audit-checklist\n93\t   acceptance criterion on the manager-binary epic.\n94\t\n95\tThe Prerequisites & Process Gates section consists of template-level\n96\t\"_Pending human review_\" placeholders on the strategy itself (architecture\n97\treview, build onboarding, etc.) — these are strategy governance gates, not scope\n98\tunknowns, and do not change the epic structure. **No Investigation epics were\n99\tcreated.**\n100\t\n101\t## Epic List\n102\t\n103\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n104\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n105\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n106\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize cloudmanager reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n107\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document and publish the structured log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n108\t\n109\t## Dependency DAG\n110\t\n111\t```mermaid\n112\tgraph TD\n113\t    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E001<br/>manager binary log standardization (P0)\"]\n114\t    E002[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E002<br/>cloudmanager log standardization (P0)\"]\n115\t    E003[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E003<br/>convention documentation + publication (P1)\"]\n116\t    E001 --> E003\n117\t    E002 --> E003\n118\t```\n119\t\n120\tArrows point from dependency to dependent: E001 must complete before E003;\n121\tE002 must complete before E003; E001 and E002 are parallel.\n122\t\n123\t## DAG Justification\n124\t\n125\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n126\t|------|------|-----------|\n127\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 | Same repo, different areas (manager vs. cloudmanager binaries, distinct deployment targets: OpenShift vs. AWS EKS / Azure AKS / CoreWeave). Parallel; merge coordination is a coordination risk, not a dependency. Splitting into two epics follows the architectural sub-system boundary axis and lets cloudmanager be scheduled independently (open question on release timing). No cross-epic artifact exists: each epic owns the CI lint/static-analysis coverage for its own binary's paths, landing with its own conversions, so neither epic consumes or modifies an artifact produced by the other (if both land, the repository ends up with lint coverage over both path sets). |\n128\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic; the guideline documents the convention as actually implemented in the manager binary. |\n129\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic; the guideline must reflect the cloudmanager conversions and shared-reconciler behavior. |\n130\t\n131\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n132\t\n133\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n134\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n135\t| All reconciliation error logs embedding resource name/namespace in message strings are converted to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) | P0 | E001, E002 | Manager scope in E001; cloudmanager scope in E002. |\n136\t| DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention | P0 | E001 | |\n137\t| Service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | |\n138\t| All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across all 4 tiers (15 files) | P0 | E001 | All 15 action files live in the manager binary scope. |\n139\t| Cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n140\t| Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 | |\n141\t| DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (queryability enhancement) | P1 | E001 | Bundled: shares the exact same `log.Error()` call sites being converted under the P0 HLRs — same surface, not independently deferrable; splitting would build the same surface twice. |\n142\t| Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | E003 | docs-authoring; priority from strategy `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception. |\n143\t\n144\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n145\t\n146\t## Supporting Documentation Dispositions\n147\t\n148\t- **Design doc (\"To be created\" in the strategy)** — its content (the\n149\t  three-problem diagnosis, before/after code examples for converting\n150\t  message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs, and the DSCI key-migration\n151\t  guide) is folded into the docs-authoring epic's contribution guideline\n152\t  rather than authored as a separate pre-implementation design doc. The\n153\t  guideline is blocked by both implementation epics so the examples are drawn\n154\t  from the actual conversions. The disposition is recorded in the docs epic's\n155\t  Risks and Notes.\n156\t\n157\t## Health Warnings\n158\t\n159\t- **DSCI key-name migration is query-breaking**: operators with existing\n160\t  Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` will\n161\t  break. Mitigation is an acceptance criterion on the manager epic: single-release\n162\t  transition, release-notes entry with before/after query examples.\n163\t- **Log consumer regression risk**: text-based alert rules may stop matching when\n164\t  message strings change. SRE/Support coordination and a before/after ingestion\n165\t  check are acceptance criteria on both implementation epics (non-blocking for\n166\t  decomposition; must be verified before merge).\n167\t- **Cloudmanager release scheduling unresolved** (PM-owned open question):\n168\t  cloudmanager work is only deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes, so impact is\n169\t  narrower; the epic is independently schedulable and may follow in a subsequent\n170\t  release without changing the manager work.\n171\t- **COO (Loki/Tempo) adoption timing unknown** and outside RHOAI control per\n172\t  platform policy overlay 0008: publication and tracking issues are in scope\n173\t  (docs epic); external adoption itself is voluntary, best-effort, and gates\n174\t  nothing.\n175\t- **Prerequisites & Process Gates** are all template \"_Pending human review_\"\n176\t  placeholders at strategy level; they are not decomposition unknowns but should\n177\t  be confirmed before implementation starts.\n178\t- **Priority note**: the P1 `resourceKind` HLR for DSC/component controllers is\n179\t  deliberately bundled into the P0 manager epic (same log-call surface, tightly\n180\t  coupled to the P0 conversion edits) — this is a coupling decision, not an\n181\t  overlooked split.\n182\t\n183\t## Tiered Delivery\n184\t\n185\tNot applicable. The only external dependency with uncertain timing (Cluster\n186\tObservability Operator team adoption of the convention) does not gate any\n187\tin-strategy deliverable: per platform policy overlay 0008, RHOAI publishes the\n188\tconvention and external adoption is voluntary. The strategy's full value\n189\t(structured, queryable operator logs) is delivered by the two implementation\n190\tepics alone; no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed.\n191\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c8744d23dc4db6f","agent_id":"a6df8657a966d6df5","session_id":"c024f609-bb75-4102-a6f5-d71433960ced","uuid":"d8902299-63be-465c-a42b-42cd03bca35a","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:27:23.409Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b11f654f0b006f5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
