{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0977641db52758f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n3\ttitle: Standardize cloudmanager reconciliation error logs with structured resource\n4\t  context\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n6\tcomponent: AI Core Platform\n7\tteam: ODH Operator team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tdependencies: []\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 7\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Standardize cloudmanager reconciliation error logs with structured resource context\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tApplies the same structured logging standardization to the cloudmanager binary\n35\tthat ships with the rhods-operator. The cloudmanager binary handles multi-cloud\n36\tinfrastructure dependencies (deployed on AWS EKS, Azure AKS, and CoreWeave) and\n37\treconciles the AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, and\n38\tCoreWeaveKubernetesEngine resources through a shared reconciler pattern that\n39\talready provides `name` and `namespace` via controller-runtime's request logger.\n40\tThis epic converts any remaining message-embedded resource identity in those\n41\tcontrollers' error paths to structured key-value pairs and adds `resourceKind`\n42\tso the infrastructure kinds are queryable like all manager-binary resources.\n43\tThe work is in the same repository as the manager binary changes but is an\n44\tarchitecturally distinct sub-system with a narrower deployment footprint\n45\t(non-OpenShift Kubernetes only), so it is separately schedulable.\n46\t\n47\t## Scope\n48\t\n49\t- Audit `log.Error()` call sites in the reconciliation error paths of the three\n50\t  cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n51\t  CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine).\n52\t- Convert any error log that embeds resource name or namespace in the message\n53\t  string to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`).\n54\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager controller\n55\t  reconciliation error logs (the kind is not implied by the shared reconciler\n56\t  pattern, so this is a functional requirement, not just queryability).\n57\t- Handle cluster-scoped infrastructure resources correctly: `name` and\n58\t  `resourceKind` present, `namespace` omitted or empty — never a spurious value.\n59\t- Add CI lint/static-analysis coverage, owned by this epic, that flags\n60\t  `log.Error` calls in the cloudmanager controller paths embedding resource\n61\t  identity in message text or missing the required structured fields. The\n62\t  coverage is scoped to the cloudmanager paths and lands together with this\n63\t  epic's conversions, so it does not depend on the manager-binary lint work;\n64\t  if both epics land, the repository ends up with lint coverage over both\n65\t  binaries' paths.\n66\t- Verify backwards compatibility for cloud deployments: before/after log output\n67\t  comparison for a sample reconciliation error and confirmation that the\n68\t  relevant log consumer (e.g. CloudWatch on cloud deployments) ingests both\n69\t  formats without configuration changes.\n70\t\n71\t## Acceptance Criteria\n72\t\n73\t- Static analysis over the cloudmanager binary reports zero\n74\t  reconciliation-error `log.Error` calls that embed resource name or namespace\n75\t  in the message text.\n76\t- Unit tests parsing test logger output assert `name`, `namespace`, and\n77\t  `resourceKind` are present with correct values in the error logs of all three\n78\t  cloudmanager controllers.\n79\t- A unit test confirms cluster-scoped infrastructure resources emit `name` and\n80\t  `resourceKind` without a spurious namespace value.\n81\t- CI lint/static-analysis coverage owned by this epic flags `log.Error` calls\n82\t  in the cloudmanager controller paths that embed resource identity in message\n83\t  text or miss the required structured fields, and flags a deliberately\n84\t  non-compliant sample call in a test.\n85\t- A before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error shows\n86\t  the structured fields present, and the cloud log consumer ingests both the\n87\t  old and new formats without configuration changes.\n88\t\n89\t## HLR Traceability\n90\t\n91\t- [P0] All cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n92\t  CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`,\n93\t  `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n94\t- [P0] All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or\n95\t  namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs\n96\t  (`name`, `namespace`) — cloudmanager binary scope.\n97\t\n98\t## Risks and Notes\n99\t\n100\t- Whether this work ships in the same release as the manager binary changes or\n101\t  in a subsequent release is an open product decision; the epic is deliberately\n102\t  independent so it can be scheduled either way without affecting the manager\n103\t  work.\n104\t- Cloudmanager is only deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure\n105\t  AKS, CoreWeave), so its log-consumer regression surface is narrower than the\n106\t  manager binary's.\n107\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c8744d23dc4db6f","agent_id":"a6df8657a966d6df5","session_id":"c024f609-bb75-4102-a6f5-d71433960ced","uuid":"76cf778e-eaf6-46af-bb80-049b363a60bd","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:27:29.567Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0977641db52758f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
